<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stamp Act Congress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1765">1765</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stamp Act Congress met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City in October 1765, with delegates from nine British American colonies, to oppose the Stamp Act. It adopted a Declaration of Rights and Grievances asserting that only the colonists' own assemblies could tax them, and sent petitions to the king and Parliament. It was the first joint colonial action against British policy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stamp-act-congress.jpg" alt="Stamp Act Congress" width="265" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: not listed · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1765_one_penny_stamp.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the French and Indian War Parliament sought more revenue from the colonies to meet the cost of stationing troops there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> and Currency Acts of 1764 raised money through trade duties, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamp-act-1765">Stamp Act 1765</a> was its first attempt at direct taxation: newspapers, legal documents, almanacs, playing cards and dice had to carry a stamp bought from a government agent, from 1 November 1765. In June 1765 the Massachusetts Assembly invited the other colonies to consult together. Massachusetts, Rhode Island, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, Delaware, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> sent delegates, while the governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> prevented their assemblies from meeting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Congress sat from 7 to 25 October 1765 in New York City Hall, now Federal Hall, behind closed doors, and little is known of its debates. It chose Timothy Ruggles of Massachusetts as chairman, narrowly rejecting James Otis, and agreed that each delegation would cast one vote. The delegates spent much time on the difference between internal taxes and the regulation of trade, and on 19 October adopted the Declaration of Rights and Grievances, whose fourteen statements professed loyalty to the Crown and held that only the colonists' own representatives could tax them.</p><p>An address to the king, a memorial to the House of Lords and a petition to the House of Commons followed on 22 and 23 October. Connecticut, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> declined to sign, and Ruggles and Robert Ogden of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> refused as well; Ruggles quarrelled with Thomas McKean, challenged him to a duel and left the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> the Lords of Trade called it a general congress appointed without the authority of the Crown, a measure of dangerous tendency. The Commons declined the petition as the work of an unconstitutional assembly, and Lord Dartmouth rejected the one to the Lords. The Rockingham Ministry, rallying merchants whose trade had suffered from colonial boycotts, carried the repeal of the Stamp Act on 18 March 1766, but Parliament passed the Declaratory Act the same day, asserting its power to make laws for the colonies in all cases whatsoever. Historians generally see the Congress as one of the first organised political actions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, though its members did not yet seek independence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stamp Act Congress” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
